I'll extend the gateway with a **custom logout handler** (clearing the Redis-backed session + Keycloak RP-Initiated Logout), **CSRF disabled**, and a **custom authentication entry point** that returns a clean JSON 401 instead of the default redirect/HTML.

Since you now want session-based behavior (Redis sessions + logout), this implies the **BFF (Backend-For-Frontend)** pattern where the gateway acts as both an **OAuth2 Client** (login via Keycloak, stores tokens in a Redis WebSession) and an **OAuth2 Resource Server**. This is exactly the scenario the Spring community recommends: The main use cases for oauth2Login are server-side rendered UIs, and Spring Cloud Gateway used as an OAuth2 Backend For Frontend.

Let me give you the additions.

---

## 1. Extra Dependencies

```xml
<!-- OAuth2 Client: enables login + session/token storage (BFF) -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client</artifactId>
</dependency>

<!-- Spring Session backed by Redis: persists WebSession across gateway instances -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.session</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-session-data-redis</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

As confirmed in the BFF reference setups, the gateway acting as both client and resource server requires both the Spring Boot OAuth2 Client Starter and the spring-security-oauth2-resource-server dependency.

---

## 2. Custom Authentication Entry Point (JSON 401)

The default behavior for an OAuth2 client triggers a redirect to the Keycloak login page — which is wrong for API/XHR calls (a common complaint is that an expired JWT just shows a 401 without a graceful flow). For API clients we return a structured JSON body.

```java
package com.acme.gateway.security;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.springframework.core.io.buffer.DataBuffer;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.security.core.AuthenticationException;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.ServerAuthenticationEntryPoint;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

@Component
public class JsonAuthenticationEntryPoint implements ServerAuthenticationEntryPoint {

    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    public JsonAuthenticationEntryPoint(ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
        this.objectMapper = objectMapper;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> commence(ServerWebExchange exchange, AuthenticationException ex) {
        var response = exchange.getResponse();
        response.setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
        response.getHeaders().setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
        // RFC 6750: tell the client a bearer token is expected
        response.getHeaders().add("WWW-Authenticate",
                "Bearer error=\"invalid_token\", error_description=\"Full authentication is required\"");

        Map<String, Object> body = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        body.put("status", 401);
        body.put("error", "Unauthorized");
        body.put("message", ex.getMessage());
        body.put("path", exchange.getRequest().getPath().value());
        body.put("timestamp", Instant.now().toString());

        byte[] bytes;
        try {
            bytes = objectMapper.writeValueAsBytes(body);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            bytes = "{\"status\":401,\"error\":\"Unauthorized\"}".getBytes();
        }
        DataBuffer buffer = response.bufferFactory().wrap(bytes);
        return response.writeWith(Mono.just(buffer));
    }
}
```

You may also want a matching `ServerAccessDeniedHandler` for 403s:

```java
package com.acme.gateway.security;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.security.access.AccessDeniedException;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.authorization.ServerAccessDeniedHandler;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.Map;

@Component
public class JsonAccessDeniedHandler implements ServerAccessDeniedHandler {

    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    public JsonAccessDeniedHandler(ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
        this.objectMapper = objectMapper;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> handle(ServerWebExchange exchange, AccessDeniedException denied) {
        var response = exchange.getResponse();
        response.setStatusCode(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
        response.getHeaders().setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
        try {
            byte[] bytes = objectMapper.writeValueAsBytes(Map.of(
                    "status", 403,
                    "error", "Forbidden",
                    "message", "You do not have permission to access this resource",
                    "path", exchange.getRequest().getPath().value(),
                    "timestamp", Instant.now().toString()));
            return response.writeWith(Mono.just(response.bufferFactory().wrap(bytes)));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return response.setComplete();
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Custom Logout Handler — Clear Redis Session + Keycloak RP-Initiated Logout

This handler does three things on logout:
1. **Invalidates the WebSession** (removes it from Redis via `WebSessionManager`).
2. **Clears the security context.**
3. **Redirects to Keycloak's `end_session_endpoint`** (RP-Initiated Logout) so the SSO session is also terminated — not just the local one.

```java
package com.acme.gateway.security;

import org.springframework.security.core.Authentication;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.authentication.OAuth2AuthenticationToken;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.registration.ClientRegistration;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.registration.ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.WebFilterExchange;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.authentication.logout.ServerLogoutHandler;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.authentication.logout.SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.util.UriComponentsBuilder;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

import java.net.URI;

/**
 * Production logout for a BFF gateway:
 *  1. Drops the Spring Session stored in Redis (session.invalidate()).
 *  2. Clears the reactive SecurityContext.
 *  3. Triggers Keycloak RP-Initiated Logout (end_session_endpoint).
 */
@Component
public class KeycloakRedisLogoutHandler implements ServerLogoutHandler {

    private static final String REGISTRATION_ID = "keycloak";

    private final ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrationRepository;
    private final SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler securityContextLogoutHandler =
            new SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler();

    private final String postLogoutRedirectUri;

    public KeycloakRedisLogoutHandler(
            ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrationRepository,
            @org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value(
                    "${app.security.post-logout-redirect-uri:https://app.acme.com}") String postLogoutRedirectUri) {
        this.clientRegistrationRepository = clientRegistrationRepository;
        this.postLogoutRedirectUri = postLogoutRedirectUri;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> logout(WebFilterExchange exchange, Authentication authentication) {
        return exchange.getExchange().getSession()
                // 1. Invalidate the Redis-backed WebSession + any custom attributes
                .flatMap(session -> {
                    // ---- put any "other stuff" cleanup here ----
                    // e.g. session.getAttributes().remove("CART"); audit logging; etc.
                    return session.invalidate();
                })
                // 2. Clear SecurityContext (delegates to Spring's default handler)
                .then(securityContextLogoutHandler.logout(exchange, authentication))
                // 3. Build Keycloak end-session redirect and apply it
                .then(Mono.defer(() -> buildKeycloakLogoutRedirect(authentication)))
                .flatMap(logoutUri -> {
                    var response = exchange.getExchange().getResponse();
                    response.setStatusCode(org.springframework.http.HttpStatus.FOUND);
                    response.getHeaders().setLocation(logoutUri);
                    return response.setComplete();
                });
    }

    private Mono<URI> buildKeycloakLogoutRedirect(Authentication authentication) {
        return clientRegistrationRepository.findByRegistrationId(REGISTRATION_ID)
                .map(registration -> {
                    String endSessionEndpoint = endSessionEndpoint(registration);
                    UriComponentsBuilder builder = UriComponentsBuilder
                            .fromUriString(endSessionEndpoint)
                            .queryParam("client_id", registration.getClientId())
                            .queryParam("post_logout_redirect_uri", postLogoutRedirectUri);

                    // Pass id_token_hint when available for a clean Keycloak logout
                    if (authentication instanceof OAuth2AuthenticationToken token
                            && token.getPrincipal() instanceof
                            org.springframework.security.oauth2.core.oidc.user.OidcUser oidcUser) {
                        builder.queryParam("id_token_hint", oidcUser.getIdToken().getTokenValue());
                    }
                    return builder.build().toUri();
                })
                // Fallback: if no end_session endpoint, just go to the app
                .switchIfEmpty(Mono.fromSupplier(() -> URI.create(postLogoutRedirectUri)));
    }

    private String endSessionEndpoint(ClientRegistration registration) {
        Object endSession = registration.getProviderDetails()
                .getConfigurationMetadata().get("end_session_endpoint");
        if (endSession != null) {
            return endSession.toString();
        }
        // Keycloak convention if metadata isn't present
        return registration.getProviderDetails().getIssuerUri()
                + "/protocol/openid-connect/logout";
    }
}
```

> **Note on Redis cleanup:** When you use `spring-session-data-redis`, calling `session.invalidate()` deletes the session key from Redis automatically through the `ReactiveSessionRepository` — you do not need to manually call `RedisTemplate.delete(...)`. If you store *additional* keys yourself (e.g. a custom cart or rate-limit bucket), inject a `ReactiveStringRedisTemplate` and delete those explicitly in the cleanup block marked above.

---

## 4. Updated Security Configuration

This wires up the **entry point**, **access denied handler**, **logout handler**, **CSRF disabled**, plus both **`oauth2Login` (client)** and **`oauth2ResourceServer` (JWT)** in a single filter chain — registering both roles in one chain is exactly the BFF approach.

```java
package com.acme.gateway.config;

import com.acme.gateway.security.JsonAccessDeniedHandler;
import com.acme.gateway.security.JsonAuthenticationEntryPoint;
import com.acme.gateway.security.KeycloakRedisLogoutHandler;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.HttpMethod;
import org.springframework.security.config.Customizer;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.reactive.EnableWebFluxSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.web.server.ServerHttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.SecurityWebFilterChain;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.authentication.logout.HttpStatusReturningServerLogoutSuccessHandler;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.util.matcher.ServerWebExchangeMatchers;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.server.resource.authentication.ReactiveJwtAuthenticationConverterAdapter;

@Configuration
@EnableWebFluxSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityWebFilterChain securityFilterChain(
            ServerHttpSecurity http,
            ReactiveJwtAuthenticationConverterAdapter jwtConverter,
            JsonAuthenticationEntryPoint authenticationEntryPoint,
            JsonAccessDeniedHandler accessDeniedHandler,
            KeycloakRedisLogoutHandler logoutHandler) {

        http
            // CSRF disabled per requirement (stateless bearer APIs; BFF uses SameSite cookies)
            .csrf(ServerHttpSecurity.CsrfSpec::disable)
            .cors(Customizer.withDefaults())

            .authorizeExchange(exchange -> exchange
                .pathMatchers("/actuator/health/**", "/actuator/info", "/actuator/prometheus").permitAll()
                .pathMatchers(HttpMethod.OPTIONS, "/**").permitAll()
                .pathMatchers("/login/**", "/oauth2/**").permitAll()
                .pathMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .pathMatchers("/api/**").authenticated()
                .anyExchange().authenticated()
            )

            // --- OAuth2 Client (BFF login; tokens stored in Redis-backed session) ---
            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())

            // --- OAuth2 Resource Server (validate Bearer JWTs from API clients) ---
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2
                .jwt(jwt -> jwt.jwtAuthenticationConverter(jwtConverter))
            )

            // --- Custom error handling ---
            .exceptionHandling(handling -> handling
                .authenticationEntryPoint(authenticationEntryPoint)
                .accessDeniedHandler(accessDeniedHandler)
            )

            // --- Custom logout: Redis session removal + Keycloak RP-Initiated Logout ---
            .logout(logout -> logout
                .logoutUrl("/logout")
                .logoutHandler(logoutHandler)
                // logoutHandler already writes the redirect, so success handler just no-ops/200
                .logoutSuccessHandler(new HttpStatusReturningServerLogoutSuccessHandler())
            )

            .headers(headers -> headers
                .frameOptions(ServerHttpSecurity.HeaderSpec.FrameOptionsSpec::disable)
            );

        return http.build();
    }
}
```

> **CSRF note:** Disabling CSRF is reasonable for pure token-based APIs. But since this BFF now also issues a **session cookie** via `oauth2Login`, the cookie is exposed to CSRF. Mitigate by setting `SameSite=Lax` (or `Strict`) and `Secure` on the session cookie (see config below). If your browser app does cross-site state-changing requests, prefer keeping CSRF enabled with `CookieServerCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse()` instead of disabling it.

---

## 5. `application.yml` Additions

```yaml
spring:
  # Spring Session -> Redis (clustered, survives restarts; logout removes the key)
  session:
    store-type: redis
    timeout: 30m
    redis:
      flush-mode: on_save
      namespace: "gateway:session"

  security:
    oauth2:
      client:
        registration:
          keycloak:
            provider: keycloak
            client-id: ${KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID:acme-gateway}
            client-secret: ${KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET}
            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code
            scope: openid,profile,email
            redirect-uri: "{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}"
        provider:
          keycloak:
            issuer-uri: ${KEYCLOAK_ISSUER_URI:https://keycloak.acme.com/realms/acme}
      resourceserver:
        jwt:
          issuer-uri: ${KEYCLOAK_ISSUER_URI:https://keycloak.acme.com/realms/acme}

app:
  security:
    post-logout-redirect-uri: ${POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI:https://app.acme.com}

server:
  reactive:
    session:
      cookie:
        same-site: lax
        secure: true
        http-only: true
```

---

## 6. Keycloak Client Setup (required for logout redirect)

For the RP-Initiated Logout redirect to work, in your Keycloak client you must register the post-logout URI as a **Valid post-logout redirect URI** — the same way you register `.../login/oauth2/code/keycloak` as a Valid redirect URI.

---

## How the Logout Flow Works End-to-End

1. Browser/app calls `POST /logout` on the gateway.
2. `KeycloakRedisLogoutHandler` runs:
   - `session.invalidate()` → **Spring Session deletes the key from Redis** (plus your custom cleanup).
   - `SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler` clears the reactive security context.
   - Builds the Keycloak `end_session_endpoint` URL with `id_token_hint` + `post_logout_redirect_uri`.
3. Gateway responds `302` → browser hits Keycloak, which ends the **SSO session**.
4. Keycloak redirects back to your `post_logout_redirect_uri`.

This gives you a **complete** logout (local Redis session + Keycloak SSO), not just a local one.

A couple of things worth confirming for your environment:
- Are your API clients **browser-based (cookies/BFF)** or **machine-to-machine (Bearer tokens)**? If purely the latter, you don't need `oauth2Login`/sessions/logout at all — logout is just token discard client-side. The session+logout machinery only matters for the BFF/browser case.
- Do you want me to add a **`ReactiveStringRedisTemplate`-based cleanup** example (deleting custom keys like cart/refresh-token mappings) or a **WireMock/Testcontainers test** for the logout redirect?

Want me to wire in the explicit Redis custom-key cleanup, or add the logout integration test next?
